  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened tripartite institutions and social dialogue processes for improved socioeconomic recovery</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO continues to be committed to improving working conditions, advancing labour rights, and promoting a culture of safety and health at work despite the dynamic political and economic landscape of the Occupied Palestinian Territory (OPT). The ongoing crisis highlights how vital it is to improve occupational safety and health (OSH) and labour inspection systems to protect worker rights and guarantee adherence to international labour standards (ILS) during these unsettling times. 
Building on its extensive experience in the OPT, the ILO has supported the assessment and development of the national OSH profile and labour inspection system. These efforts have provided a roadmap for improvement in line with worldwide best practices. Previous initiatives have advanced labour inspection and occupational safety and health, as evidenced by the implementation of gender-responsive inspector training and the establishment of the first Palestinian Labour Inspection (LI) Conference, which served as a forum for addressing system gaps and sharing successful regional practices. 
The comprehensive assessment of the LI system, which aimed to support the implementation of a reform plan in accordance with the Labour Inspection Convention of 1947 (No. 81), paved the way for the draft National Labour Inspection Policy and Strategy, as well as a draft National Policy on Occupational Safety and Health. These documents, created with ILO support, are critical for advancing the labour rights agenda in the OPT and will pave the way for labour inspector capacity building, as well as the enhancement of strategic compliance mechanisms through the implementation of new tools and practices. 
The ILO is now working closely with the Labour Inspection and OSH Directorate to develop a capacity-building and training plan, to complete the draft National Labour Inspection Policy and Strategy. With this approach, inspectors will be able to overcome capacity issues and successfully carry out their assi</narrative>
  </description>
